// =============================================================================
// TAILNET POLICY — OMEGA ENGINE DEFINITIVE (GRANTS + HUMAN-ONLY SSH)
// Date: 2026-09-30 | By: MaKaLi Fusion for the Architect
// Supersedes: tailnet-policy-OMEGA-DEFINITIVE-20260926.hujson (v1)
//            the 8017->8019 change of 2026-09-28    (v2)
//            THIS FILE IS THE SOLE AUTHORITATIVE POLICY. There is no version
//            chain and no superseded sibling — that arrangement is what produced
//            "three policy files, no marker of which is authoritative", and a
//            single file with its history in this header is the fix.
// =============================================================================
//
// THE TWO PORTS, AND WHY BOTH. They are not versions of one thing and must
// never be merged.
//
//   8016  omega-hub, FastMCP.        LIVE CONVERSATION.
//         Reactive. A request comes in, an answer goes out, latency matters,
//         someone is waiting.
//
//   8019  omega-exchange, read-only.  FILE TRANSFER.
//         Durable. You fetch, you verify the sha256, it works at 3am or it
//         does not.
//
//   THE HIGHWAY RULE: nothing moves that isn't a file, and no file moves that
//   isn't hash-verified. Conversations stay on 8016. Everything else —
//   packages, briefings, research, WADs, archives, reports, lessons — is a file
//   on 8019. Merging the two gives either an unreliable file channel or a
//   chatty one. Both nodes run the SAME services on the SAME ports; the ports
//   are per-host, so 8019 here and 8019 there are different sockets.
//
// CHANGES FROM v1 (2026-09-26) — five, all federation-driven:
//
//   1. GRANT  tcp:8017 -> tcp:8019
//      8017 was SearXNG all along; the exchange pipe moved to 8019 on
//      2026-09-27. The 8017 Serve route has been REMOVED (tailscale serve
//      --https=8017 off) so SearXNG cannot be re-armed by a policy edit.
//
//   2. GRANT  N0 -> N1 gains tcp:8019.  <-- NEW, 2026-09-30
//      Until now the tailnet was a ONE-WAY STREET. Node 1 could reach Node 0 on
//      8016 + 8019 — proven by 114 access-logged pulls including a 24-file,
//      455,778-byte package verified 24/24 from Node 1's own vantage. Node 0
//      could reach Node 1 on 8016 only, so the REVERSE direction had never been
//      exercised by anyone. The Architect's ruling on 2026-09-30 is that the
//      federation is duplex. This is a change of INTENT, deliberately recorded,
//      not a widened blast radius.
//
//   3. TEST   accept lists made SYMMETRIC, both directions asserting 8016+8019.
//      Tailscale REJECTS a policy whose tests do not match its rules, so this
//      was mandatory with change 2. v2's tests were asymmetric; asymmetry meant
//      duplex was a comment rather than a proved property.
//
//   4. TEST   deny assertions completed in BOTH directions for all five closed
//      doors: 22, 2049, 8017, 6379, 51372. A deny test means a future policy
//      edit that re-adds one is REFUSED AT SAVE TIME instead of silently
//      widening the blast radius. v2 denied 8017 and 6379 in one direction only
//      and 51372 in the other, which meant neither direction was fully guarded.
//
//   5. COMMENTS corrected and completed. The 8017 grant comment no longer claims
//      it was "the MISSING rule" when it was never the right port, and the
//      805 proposal Node 1 raised is recorded below as NOT adopted, with why.
//
// NOT ADOPTED — the 805 Node 1 proposed, and why:
//   Not a collision. Ports are PER-HOST: 8019 on 100.123.51.67 and 8019 on
//   100.89.40.17 are different sockets. 805 was an artefact of the plane/stack
//   architecture, where each plane is a separately bound socket on one host and
//   distinct ports are how you bind them apart. Both nodes bind 8019 and run
//   the IDENTICAL omega_exchange_server.py — same port, same code.
//
// UNCHANGED FROM v1, and each one load-bearing:
//   - All node-to-node SSH removed, both planes (ssh rules AND tcp:22 grants).
//   - Human-operator-only Tailscale SSH, action=check, default 12h period.
//   - NFS 2049 removed.
//   - ICMP both directions.
//   - No funnel, no autoApprovers, no allow-all, deny-by-default.
//   - 8019 and 8016 backends stay bound to 127.0.0.1. NEVER 0.0.0.0.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY tag->tag SSH CANNOT BE IN CHECK MODE  (unchanged, still load-bearing)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tailscale permits exactly four kinds of Tailscale SSH connection:
//   - user -> their own devices
//   - user -> a tagged device
//   - user -> a shared tagged device
//   - tagged device -> another tagged device   <-- CANNOT be check mode
//
// Check mode requires periodic re-auth against an identity provider. A tagged
// device has no user identity to re-authenticate as, so tag->tag SSH is
// STRUCTURALLY forced to "accept" -- meaning NO periodic re-auth, ever. There
// is no policy setting that changes this. Accept it or remove the rule.
//
// The tailnet policy is NOT the whole exposure. On 2026-09-28 S1 verified the
// host-layer reality behind this: `ss -ltn` showed rpcbind:111 and statd:662
// still wildcard-bound, and a DLNA server (rygel) on seven non-loopback
// addresses including the LAN. `make check-lan-exposure` exists to see the part
// the policy cannot describe.
//
// WHAT YOU KEEP: human operator (autogroup:admin) -> tag:node0, tag:node1,
// action "check". src is autogroup:admin, NOT a tag -- that is the entire
// point: a user identity can re-authenticate; a tag cannot.
// =============================================================================

{
	"tagOwners": {
		"tag:node0":    ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:node1":    ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:opencode": ["autogroup:admin"],
	},

	// allow-all intentionally ABSENT. Deny-by-default for anything unlisted.
	"grants": [
		// --- SYMMETRIC DUPLEX: MCP bridge + exchange, BOTH directions -------
		// 8016 = omega-hub FastMCP.        8019 = read-only exchange pipe.
		// Same port number, same service, each bound to its own loopback and
		// fronted by its own Tailscale Serve. Keep both on 127.0.0.1 -- do NOT
		// bind 0.0.0.0. Unblock proof, from Node 1:
		//   curl -sk https://n0.tail51f14a.ts.net:8019/manifest.json
		{"src": ["tag:node0"], "dst": ["tag:node1"], "ip": ["tcp:8016", "tcp:8019"]},
		{"src": ["tag:node1"], "dst": ["tag:node0"], "ip": ["tcp:8016", "tcp:8019"]},

		// --- (removed) tcp:8017 ---------------------------------------------
		// 8017 is the SearXNG web service. It was never the exchange pipe in
		// this policy; the exchange pipe is 8019. The 8017 Serve route was
		// removed on 2026-09-28 because SearXNG is an unauthenticated
		// outbound-proxy surface with SSRF and abuse-as-a-proxy
		// characteristics. Do not re-add. A deny test below guards it both ways.

		// --- (removed) tcp:6379 (Redis) -------------------------------------
		// omega-redis container, quadlet and volume deleted 2026-09-28. Guarded.

		// --- (removed) tcp:51372 (Deluge) -----------------------------------
		// deluged was bound to wlo1 AND tailscale0. Guarded.

		// --- (removed) tcp:2049 (NFSv4) -------------------------------------
		// Removed under D-FED-01. Host service disabled 2026-09-28; rpcbind
		// and rpc-statd were later masked. Guarded both ways.

		// --- (removed) tag:opencode -> tag:node1 tcp:22 ---------------------
		// Automation SSH. The same ungoverned side door, differently named.

		// --- (removed) tag:node1 -> tag:node0 tcp:22 -----------------------
		// Regular OpenSSH over WireGuard -- a separate plane from the "ssh"
		// section below. No port-22 path between nodes at all.

		// --- ICMP / ping, both directions -----------------------------------
		{"src": ["tag:node0"], "dst": ["tag:node1"], "ip": ["icmp:*"]},
		{"src": ["tag:node1"], "dst": ["tag:node0"], "ip": ["icmp:*"]},
	],

	// --- SSH: HUMAN OPERATOR ONLY -------------------------------------------
	"ssh": [
		{
			// checkPeriod is intentionally ABSENT. Tailscale rejected a custom
			// value: "Functionality outside your plan: custom 'checkPeriod' is
			// only available on the Premium or Enterprise plans." Omitting it
			// applies the default of 12h, which is correct for this tier. Do not
			// re-add unless the tailnet is upgraded. If you later move to
			// Premium/Enterprise, add back e.g. "checkPeriod": "4h".
			"action": "check",
			"src":    ["autogroup:admin"],
			"dst":    ["tag:node0", "tag:node1"],
			"users":  ["autogroup:nonroot", "root"],
		},
	],

	// Funnel node attribute: REMOVED (public-internet exposure; unused).
	// autoApprovers: OMITTED (no subnet routes, no exit nodes).

	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// POLICY TESTS -- a failing assertion makes Tailscale REJECT the whole file.
	// src/accept/deny must name specific entities; "*" is not permitted here.
	// These are not documentation: every grant is proved and every closed door
	// is proved shut, in BOTH directions.
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"tests": [
		{
			// Both nodes reach both ports on the other. Symmetric, because
			// asymmetry is what made duplex a comment in v2.
			"src":    "tag:node1",
			"accept": ["tag:node0:8016", "tag:node0:8019"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:node0",
			"accept": ["tag:node1:8016", "tag:node1:8019"],
		},
		{
			// REGRESSION GUARD -- no cross-node SSH, no NFS, and no path back to
			// the three services removed on 2026-09-28. If anyone re-adds any of
			// these, the save is REFUSED by Tailscale.
			"src":  "tag:node1",
			"deny": ["tag:node0:22", "tag:node0:2049",
			         "tag:node0:8017", "tag:node0:6379", "tag:node0:51372"],
		},
		{
			"src":  "tag:node0",
			"deny": ["tag:node1:22", "tag:node1:2049", "tag:node1:8017",
			         "tag:node1:6379", "tag:node1:51372"],
		},
	],
}
